     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this one as written, even with your confirmation. 1600 mg four times daily is 6400 mg/day, which is twice the maximum daily dose and twice the maximum single dose. I don't know of any accepted adult indication for oral ibuprofen at this level. At this dose there's a real risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury and other toxicity, so I don't think a confirmation alone is enough for me to put it in.\n\n**Ways forward:**\n- **If there's a clinical reason** (a specific protocol, a research study, or advice from a specialist or toxicologist), tell me what it is. I'll enter the order with that reason and a flag for pharmacist verification.\n- **If the pain isn't controlled enough**, I can enter ibuprofen at up to 800 mg four times daily (3200 mg/day). You could also add a non-NSAID drug, like scheduled acetaminophen, or something else you choose.\n- **You can also talk to the ward pharmacist directly**, or enter the order yourself if your institution allows a documented override. I don't want to stand in the way of your decision. I'm just not comfortable being the one to submit this dose without a reason on record.\n\nWhat would you like to do?"
      }
     ],
